Confirmed: `b45c4545d` is the direct parent of the fix commit and is the commit that introduced the entire `loadUserTypes` method — including the buggy logic.

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: b45c4545d2f4027e77dfb4fe856c42f51d897c08

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** I traced the history of `loadUserTypes` in `BulkLoader.java` using `git log --follow -p`. The function only appears in two commits: it was newly introduced in `b45c4545d` and then modified by the fix commit `a4ec18f80` (which is its direct parent in the log). No other commits touch this function, so the bug must originate in the commit that created it.

**(2) The specific defect:** In `b45c4545d`, `loadUserTypes` was written as:
```java
private static void loadUserTypes(Collection<UserType> udts, KSMetaData ks) {
    LinkedList<UserType> notLoaded = new LinkedList<UserType>();
    for (UserType ut : udts) { ... }
    if (notLoaded.size() == udts.size()) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Unable to load user types " + notLoaded);
    }
    if (notLoaded.size() > 0) {
        loadUserTypes(notLoaded, ks);
    }
}
```
If `udts` is empty (e.g., a keyspace with no user-defined types), the `for` loop never executes, so `notLoaded` stays empty. The check `notLoaded.size() == udts.size()` then evaluates `0 == 0`, which is `true`, causing the method to incorrectly throw `RuntimeException("Unable to load user types []")` even though there was nothing to fail on. This means any bulk-load of a keyspace without UDTs would fail with a spurious exception.

**(3) Evidence for the fix matching this root cause:** The fix commit `a4ec18f80` adds exactly the missing guard:
```java
if (udts.isEmpty()) {
    return;
}
```
at the top of the method, directly preventing the vacuous "0 == 0" failure case introduced in `b45c4545d`. It also simplifies the final recursive call to be unconditional, relying on the new early-return guard to terminate recursion safely — consistent with fixing the same root defect (the flawed empty-collection handling introduced when the method was first written).

I'm confident in this attribution since the function's entire lifecycle spans only these two commits, and the fix directly patches the logical flaw present since the method's introduction.

